{"parentUuid":"673398ff-9d49-425c-9adc-23cd60594432","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LZFt99v3qHgdhYex5RsQVi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SCS solver first-order SDP large scale GPU 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"PDCS: A Primal-Dual Large-Scale Conic Programming Solver with GPU Enhancements\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.00311v1\"},{\"title\":\"SCS on multiple GPU's · Issue #98 · cvxgrp/scs\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvxgrp/scs/issues/98\"},{\"title\":\"JOTA manuscript No. (will be inserted by the editor)\",\"url\":\"https://stanford.edu/~boyd/papers/pdf/scs_long.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A user manual for cuHALLaR: A GPU accelerated low-rank semidefinite programming Solver – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/2025/08/a-user-manual-for-cuhallar-a-gpu-accelerated-low-rank-semidefinite-programming-solver/\"},{\"title\":\"Readme · SCS.jl\",\"url\":\"https://docs.juliahub.com/General/SCS/stable/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - cvxgrp/scs: Splitting Conic Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvxgrp/scs\"},{\"title\":\"Practical first order methods for large scale semidefinite ...\",\"url\":\"https://stephentu.github.io/writeups/first-order-sdp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"jump-dev/SCS.jl · JuMP\",\"url\":\"https://jump.dev/JuMP.jl/stable/packages/SCS/\"},{\"title\":\"SCS — SCS 3.2.11 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxgrp.org/scs/\"},{\"title\":\"SCS - YALMIP\",\"url\":\"https://yalmip.github.io/solver/scs/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about SCS solver, first-order methods for SDP, and large-scale GPU optimization in 2025:\n\n## SCS Solver Overview\n\nSCS (Splitting Conic Solver) is a numerical optimization package for solving large-scale convex quadratic cone problems. SCS employs an ADMM-based approach to solve the homogeneous self-dual embedding.\n\n## GPU Implementation\n\nThe ADMM-based solver SCS has a GPU implementation that leverages the conjugate gradient method for solving linear systems. To enable the indirect linear solver on GPU one needs to install and load SCS_GPU_jll, and the GpuIndirectSolver is available on Linux x86_64 platform only.\n\n## First-Order Methods for Large-Scale SDP\n\nIn recent 2025 research, for SDP problems, recent works propose a less expensive GPU-based first-order solver cuLoRADS that is based on the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) and the Burer-Monterio method. It does not require projections onto the cone and exhibits better performance than commercial solvers in solving many large-scale SDP problem instances.\n\n## Scalability Considerations\n\nADMM-based methods require solving a linear system in each iteration, which is typically handled through matrix factorization or the conjugate gradient method, but matrix factorizations suffer from scalability limitations and are not well-suited for parallel computing on GPUs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"db12ff99-69d7-4b61-88cc-354486556c9e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:25.466Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"673398ff-9d49-425c-9adc-23cd60594432","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9bff0c85-cb45-4bf3-a07b-e70e5199ac3e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KCLaA9bZVxcNs9f3bJhL5j","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"spectral relaxation graph placement Cheeger inequality bound combinatorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Four proofs for the Cheeger inequality and graph partition ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.math.pku.edu.cn/teachers/yaoy/Fall2011/cheeger_chung.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 4 Cheeger's Inequality\",\"url\":\"https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~lapchi/cs860-2022/notes/04-Cheeger.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Allow Me to Introduce Spectral and Isoperimetric Graph Partitioning\",\"url\":\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~jrs/papers/partnotes.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Cheeger's Inequality and the Sparsest Cut Problem\",\"url\":\"https://homes.cs.washington.edu/~shayan/courses/approx/adv-approx-17.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Laplacians and the Cheeger inequality for directed graphs ∗ Fan Chung†\",\"url\":\"https://people.cs.umass.edu/~mahadeva/cs791bb/reading/dichee.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GRAPH PARTITIONING AND MULTI-WAY CHEEGER INEQUALITIES WILLIAM HU\",\"url\":\"http://math.uchicago.edu/~may/REU2023/REUPapers/Hu,William.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Spectral Graph Theory, Cheeger's inequality\",\"url\":\"https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse521/16sp/521-lecture-12.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Multiway Spectral Partitioning and Higher-Order Cheeger Inequalities | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51958710_Multiway_Spectral_Partitioning_and_Higher-Order_Cheeger_Inequalities\"},{\"title\":\"Lecture 15: Cheeger's Inequality cont., Spectral Clustering\",\"url\":\"https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse521/20au/521-lecture-14.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Cheeger Inequality for the Graph Connection Laplacian\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/224053862_A_Cheeger_Inequality_for_the_Graph_Connection_Laplacian\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found comprehensive information about the interconnected topics of spectral relaxation, graph placement, the Cheeger inequality, and combinatorial optimization. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Spectral Relaxation and Graph Partitioning\n\nRelaxation turns a combinatorial optimization problem into a numerical optimization problem, and the relaxed problems can be solved by polynomial-time algorithms. After solving a relaxed problem, the real-valued solution is converted to an approximately optimal solution of the original graph partitioning problem by rounding the vertex values.\n\n## Cheeger's Inequality and Bounds\n\nAn attractive feature of spectral partitioning is that a bound called Cheeger's inequality guarantees that you can always find a decently good cut. Cheeger's inequality relates the second smallest eigenvalue of a graph's normalized Laplacian matrix to graph conductance, with the first inequality called the easy direction and the second inequality called the hard direction.\n\nThe lower bound argument shows that the variational representation of λ₂ is a relaxation of minimum conductance, while the upper bound requires proving the existence of a sparse cut through a constructive algorithmic approach.\n\n## Practical Applications\n\nThe Cheeger inequality has many applications, including in spectral clustering, the construction of expander graphs, approximate coloring, and image segmentation. Cheeger's inequality gives upper and lower bounds of edge expansion in normal graphs in terms of the second eigenvalue of the graph's Laplacian operator.\n\n## Spectral Partitioning Algorithm\n\nA popular heuristic for finding sparse cuts involves the spectral partitioning algorithm, which computes the second smallest eigenvalue λ₂ of the Laplacian and a corresponding eigenvector, then sorts the vertices according to the eigenvector values.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9e920a32-821d-45e5-95b3-c2c05538841f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:26.852Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9bff0c85-cb45-4bf3-a07b-e70e5199ac3e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4a1a3306-fb79-4935-8e2c-71cd988f3bad","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CXgUXiobfvzethjoeNKLdv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SDP vs LP relaxation strength tightness facility location combinatorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"On the Strength of Linear Relaxations in Ordered Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09166\"},{\"title\":\"A Novel SDP Relaxation for the Quadratic Assignment Problem Using Cut Pseudo Bases | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-45587-7_36\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Semideﬁnite Programming (SDP) Robert M. Freund March, 2004 1\",\"url\":\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-084j-nonlinear-programming-spring-2004/a632b565602fd2eb3be574c537eea095_lec23_semidef_opt.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Power of Semideﬁnite Programming Relaxations for MAX-SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~lleahu/papers/mine/cp-ai-or06/39900104.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the tightness of SDP relaxations of QCQPs ∗\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/11/7487.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Semidefinite programming - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semidefinite_programming\"},{\"title\":\"16 Introduction to Semidefinite Programming (SDP)\",\"url\":\"https://users.math.msu.edu/users/iwenmark/Teaching/MTH995/Papers/SDP_notes_Marina_Epelman_UM.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2003.09.15.01 Semideﬁnite Programming Relaxations and\",\"url\":\"https://www.mit.edu/~parrilo/cdc03_workshop/ejc03_comp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improving the Semi-Lagrangian Relaxation Approach to ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/staff/letchfoa/articles/splp-slr.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the Power of Symmetric LP and SDP Relaxations James R. Lee\",\"url\":\"https://www.dsteurer.org/paper/symsdp.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding the comparison between SDP and LP relaxations in terms of strength and tightness for combinatorial optimization and facility location:\n\n## Key Findings on SDP vs LP Relaxation Strength\n\n**Superior Tightness of SDP Relaxations:**\n\nSDP has gained considerable importance in the context of combinatorial optimization since it has been shown that it leads to tighter relaxations than those based on LP for several combinatorial problems. SDP has been shown to provide very good approximations for several combinatorial problems, in particular for the stable set problem, for the maximum cut problem and for MAX-SAT.\n\n**Practical Advantages:**\n\nIn many instances, the SDP relaxation is very tight in practice, and in certain instances in particular, the optimal solution to the SDP relaxation can be converted to a feasible solution for the original problem with provably good objective value.\n\n**Performance Comparison for MAX-SAT:**\n\nIn the overconstrained area, the upper bound based on the SDP relaxation is considerably tighter than the one provided by the LP relaxation.\n\n**Facility Location Context:**\n\nThe search results mention that facility location is among applications where QCQPs (quadratic constrained quadratic programs) arise naturally, and these problems can be addressed with SDP relaxations. Additionally, an extensive empirical study on benchmark instances from facility location evaluates in practice the quality of the LP relaxations for several problem variants.\n\nOverall, the evidence indicates that SDP relaxations generally provide stronger (tighter) bounds than LP relaxations for many combinatorial optimization problems, though the actual strength depends on the specific problem structure and instance characteristics.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"127a4f0a-97f3-40a0-b96f-a5453610a487","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:27.149Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4a1a3306-fb79-4935-8e2c-71cd988f3bad","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d53bf3b8-ac26-4ef2-8ce4-29e314d9fe82","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012hziJmfN53WphvcgmLSA1j","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CVXPY SCS Mosek SDP scalability benchmark 10000 variables 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Solver Features -\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/tutorial/solvers/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"SDP Problem Reduction High Memory Use for MOSEK · Issue #2051 · cvxpy/cvxpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvxpy/cvxpy/issues/2051\"},{\"title\":\"MOSEK-based CVX versus CVXPY Speed Issue (and CVXPY reduction chain) · cvxpy/cvxpy · Discussion #2087\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvxpy/cvxpy/discussions/2087\"},{\"title\":\"CVXPY: A Python-Embedded Modeling Language for Convex Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/301847991_CVXPY_A_Python-Embedded_Modeling_Language_for_Convex_Optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Changes to CVXPY -\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/updates/\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Features -\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/tutorial/advanced/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Features — CVXPY 1.1.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/version/1.1/tutorial/advanced/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Problems -\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/api_reference/cvxpy.problems.html\"},{\"title\":\"Changes to CVXPY — CVXPY 1.5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/updates/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Cvxpy\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, I found information about CVXPY and its solvers (SCS and MOSEK), but the search did not return any specific benchmark results comparing SDP scalability with 10,000 variables in 2025.\n\nHowever, here's what I found about these solvers and their SDP performance characteristics:\n\n## Memory Efficiency Comparison\n\nSolving large SDPs requires significantly more memory to set up in MOSEK compared to other methods such as SCS. For a 150×150 semidefinite matrix variable, MOSEK requires several GB of memory during problem compilation, whereas SCS uses virtually none.\n\n## Solver Capabilities\n\nCVXPY supports SCS and MOSEK among many other solvers, allowing you to set solver options such as the maximum number of iterations. Both solvers can handle SDP problems, though they may have different performance characteristics depending on problem formulation.\n\n## Resources for Benchmarking\n\nNotably, the CVXPY developers are seeking help to benchmark CVXPY solvers and welcome contributions of problems and results to the Solver Benchmarks project.\n\nIf you're looking for specific 2025 benchmark data with 10,000 variables for SDP problems, it doesn't appear to be publicly available in the search results. You may want to check the CVXPY GitHub repository's benchmarking projects or recent papers on SDP solver performance for the most current comparisons.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cf37e221-3c0c-429a-ad4c-ee225d791903","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:27.947Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d53bf3b8-ac26-4ef2-8ce4-29e314d9fe82","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d634f43b-e31b-4ddc-9b17-f26e168cc5f9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016biNapRKxVkfGRzQW82uYg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"second-order cone relaxation rectangle packing 2D bin packing tighter than LP\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Positions and covering: A two-stage methodology to obtain optimal solutions for the 2d-bin packing problem | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0229358\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ktnr/BinPacking2D: Exact solutions for two-dimensional bin packing problems by branch-and-cut · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ktnr/BinPacking2D\"},{\"title\":\"Survey for 2-D packing algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://www.csc.liv.ac.uk/~epa/surveyhtml.html\"},{\"title\":\"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Solved by Multi-Level Recursive Logic-based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239\"},{\"title\":\"Bin packing problem - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bin_packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - secnot/rectpack: Python 2D rectangle packing library · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/secnot/rectpack\"},{\"title\":\"On the 2D Demand Bin Packing Problem: Hardness and Approximation Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2508.13347v1\"},{\"title\":\"The two-dimensional bin packing problem with variable bin sizes and costs - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1572528605000216\"},{\"title\":\"Online calculator: 2D Bin Packing Problem Solver\",\"url\":\"https://planetcalc.com/8634/\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0377221720306111\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search for your query about second-order cone relaxation, rectangle packing, 2D bin packing, and tightness compared to linear programming relaxations.\n\nThe search results provide general information about 2D bin packing problems and various exact solution techniques. However, the results don't specifically address second-order cone (SOCP) relaxations for rectangle packing or comparisons of SOCP relaxations being tighter than LP relaxations.\n\nThe search results do mention several relevant approaches to 2D bin packing:\n\n- Set-covering formulations strengthened with valid inequalities\n- Lagrangian relaxation approaches for integer linear programming models and binary integer programming formulations\n- Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition to obtain lower bounds through LP-relaxation and branch-and-price algorithms\n- One-dimensional contiguous bin packing relaxations as lower bounds\n\nHowever, I did not find specific research results in these results about second-order cone relaxations being used for rectangle packing or whether SOCP relaxations provide tighter bounds than LP relaxations for this problem. To find more targeted information, you might want to search academic databases like arXiv, Google Scholar, or specific optimization journals that focus on semidefinite and conic programming approaches to packing problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a9c1e227-e725-461f-b4c6-6031cb6bb833","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:31.343Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d634f43b-e31b-4ddc-9b17-f26e168cc5f9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
