{"parentUuid":"eb740020-1820-48d8-a5e7-82ac65622a45","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01T51q7qA6NHSqa6vBm3NHTa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D34BRJhC1JyqrLvLCvB845","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239","prompt":"Summarize the approach used in \"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Solved by Multi-Level Recursive Logic-based Benders Decomposition\". What relaxations are used (LP, SDP, others)? Does it use SDP at any level? What problem sizes are tested?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5038,"cache_read_input_tokens":35224,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5038,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox6CptG8LnE5k9LM8no","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa0a467c-ed8e-4379-b214-0f3a68dd930c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:57.252Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fa0a467c-ed8e-4379-b214-0f3a68dd930c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01T51q7qA6NHSqa6vBm3NHTa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01H7RrCqug6cMDhBFaaac23S","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"sparse SDP\" chordal decomposition large scale solver 2025"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5038,"cache_read_input_tokens":35224,"output_tokens":369,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5038},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":369,"cache_read_input_tokens":35224,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5038,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox6CptG8LnE5k9LM8no","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9676d3fd-70a3-4bbd-a473-e6bf629c32a1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:57.881Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"eb740020-1820-48d8-a5e7-82ac65622a45","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Dihzp3zCQRRrYFM66HAsV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SDP relaxation 5000 variables computation time interior point method 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A Relaxed Interior Point Method for Low-Rank Semidefinite Programming Problems with Applications to Matrix Completion | Journal of Scientific Computing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10915-021-01654-1\"},{\"title\":\"A Relaxed Interior Point Method for Low-Rank Semidefinite Programming Problems with Applications to Matrix Completion - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8504795/\"},{\"title\":\"Interior Point Methods in Semidefinite Programming with Applications to Combinatorial Optimization | SIAM Journal on Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/0805002\"},{\"title\":\"Regularization Methods for SDP Relaxations in Large ...\",\"url\":\"https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~njw/PUBLICPAPERS/regpop.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the numerical solution of Lasserre relaxations of unconstrained binary quadratic optimization problem | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10898-025-01523-3\"},{\"title\":\"Semidefinite programming - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semidefinite_programming\"},{\"title\":\"ADMM for the SDP relaxation of the QAP\",\"url\":\"https://xu-yangyang.github.io/ADMM_QAP/\"},{\"title\":\"Interior-point algorithms for semidefinite programming ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.seas.ucla.edu/~vandenbe/publications/kyp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Full article: Loraine – an interior-point solver for low-rank semidefinite programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10556788.2023.2250522\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A relaxed interior point method for low-rank semidefinite programming problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335833521_A_relaxed_interior_point_method_for_low-rank_semidefinite_programming_problems\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about SDP relaxation computation with interior point methods. Here's what the research shows:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Computational Challenges with Interior Point Methods:**\n\nWhen interior-point methods are used, typically only small or moderately large problems could be solved. Additionally, the SDP relaxation for the QAP becomes very large, and traditional methods such as the interior-point method can hardly solve problems of even medium size, say 30.\n\n**Recent Advances (2025):**\n\nA 2025 paper addresses solving large SDP relaxation problems using a hybrid approach. The aim of this paper is to solve linear semidefinite programs arising from higher-order Lasserre relaxations of unconstrained binary quadratic optimization problems. For this they use an interior point method with a preconditioned conjugate gradient. As a result, they can solve much larger problems than standard SDP solvers applied to formulation (2).\n\n**Alternative Approaches:**\n\nBy regularization methods, significantly bigger problems could be solved on a regular computer, which is almost impossible by interior point methods.\n\n**Low-Rank Interior Point Methods:**\n\nFor problems with specific structure, a new code for the solution of large-and-sparse linear semidefinite programs (SDPs) with low-rank solutions or solutions with few outlying eigenvalues, and/or problems with low-rank data is proposed, which uses a preconditioned conjugate gradient method within an interior-point SDP algorithm.\n\nThe search results indicate that solving SDPs with thousands of variables remains computationally challenging, and researchers have developed specialized techniques including preconditioned conjugate gradient methods, regularization approaches, and low-rank solvers to handle larger instances.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cc668508-9351-4591-a760-09ea030df363","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:02.620Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"eb740020-1820-48d8-a5e7-82ac65622a45","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fa0a467c-ed8e-4379-b214-0f3a68dd930c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D34BRJhC1JyqrLvLCvB845","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary of the Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Approach\n\n## Core Approach\n\nThe paper proposes a **multi-level Logic-based Benders Decomposition (LBBD)** method for solving hierarchical rectangle packing problems. The approach recursively decomposes the problem by treating parent blocks as \"master problems\" and child blocks as \"subproblems,\" dynamically refining dimension constraints through iterative cuts.\n\n## Relaxations Used\n\nThe method employs **Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP)** and **Constraint Programming (CP)** formulations:\n\n- **MILP Model**: Uses relative-position constraints with big-M formulations and piecewise linear approximations of hyperbolic area constraints\n- **CP Model**: Employs interval variables and cumulative resource constraints\n- **No SDP**: The paper does not mention semidefinite programming at any level\n\nThe area constraint \"W×H ≥ LBarea\" is approximated as \"piecewise linear\" for MILP, but this is a convex relaxation rather than SDP.\n\n## Problem Sizes Tested\n\nThe experimental evaluation covers:\n\n- **Instance complexity**: Up to 7 hierarchy levels with average 23.44 blocks per instance\n- **Scale**: Up to 402.72 average total objects per instance\n- **Per-block size**: 5-80 rectangles per block (40-80 for single-level, reduced for deeper hierarchies)\n- **Computation time**: 10 minutes for simple instances to 4 hours for complex 7-level instances\n\nResults demonstrate the method \"outperforms both monolithic formulations and the Bottom-Up method\" on these synthetic benchmark instances."}]},"uuid":"e53c0948-9cb5-49bd-a94d-b00f830fa410","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:03.180Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa0a467c-ed8e-4379-b214-0f3a68dd930c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9676d3fd-70a3-4bbd-a473-e6bf629c32a1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a7db41848a47c8250","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H7RrCqug6cMDhBFaaac23S","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"sparse SDP\" chordal decomposition large scale solver 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Chordal decomposition in operator-splitting methods for sparse semidefinite programs | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-019-01366-3\"},{\"title\":\"Chordal sparsity in control and optimization of large-scale systems - ORA - Oxford University Research Archive\",\"url\":\"https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:dbf77067-15a0-446a-a909-f9d262e39821\"},{\"title\":\"Chordal Sparsity for SDP-based Neural Network Verification\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2206.03482\"},{\"title\":\"[1707.05058] Chordal decomposition in operator-splitting methods for sparse semidefinite programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.05058\"},{\"title\":\"Chordal decomposition in operator-splitting methods for ...\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10107-019-01366-3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Chordal Decomposition for Spectral Coarsening\",\"url\":\"https://www.dgp.toronto.edu/projects/chordal-spectral-coarsening/chordal-decomposition-for-spectral-coarsening-siggraph-asia-2020-chen-et-al-compressed.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Decomposition Methods for Large-Scale Semidefinite Programs with Chordal Aggregate Sparsity and Partial Orthogonality | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-97478-1_3\"},{\"title\":\"[2206.03482] Chordal Sparsity for SDP-based Neural Network Verification\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.03482\"},{\"title\":\"Chordal Sparsity in Control and Optimization of Large-scale Systems Yang Zheng\",\"url\":\"https://zhengy09.github.io/zheng_thesis.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Chordal decomposition in operator-splitting methods for sparse semidefinite programs\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318488327_Chordal_decomposition_in_operator-splitting_methods_for_sparse_semidefinite_programs\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found substantial information about sparse SDP solvers using chordal decomposition. Here's an overview of the key findings:\n\n## Chordal Decomposition for Sparse SDP Solvers\n\nChordal decomposition is used to reformulate large sparse semidefinite programs (SDPs), either in primal or dual standard form, into equivalent SDPs with smaller positive semidefinite (PSD) constraints.\n\n### Key Methods and Approaches\n\nThe decomposed SDP is suitable for the application of first-order operator-splitting methods, enabling efficient and scalable algorithms, particularly the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) for solving decomposed primal- and dual-standard-form SDPs. Each iteration requires a projection onto an affine subspace and a set of projections onto small PSD cones that can be computed in parallel.\n\n### The CDCS Solver\n\nCDCS is the first open-source first-order solver that exploits chordal decomposition and can detect infeasible problems. Efficient algorithms based on ADMM have been developed for sparse SDPs in either primal or dual standard form, and for their homogeneous self-dual embedding, implemented in the open-source conic solver CDCS.\n\n### Applications\n\nRecent applications of chordal decomposition include:\n- Leveraging chordal sparsity to decompose large linear matrix inequalities (LMIs) into equivalent collections of smaller LMIs for SDP-based neural network verification.\n- Decomposition methods that exploit the properties of chordal graphs and sparse positive semidefinite matrices can scale problems to much larger instances.\n\nThe search results primarily contain publications from 2017-2024, but do not include specific 2025 solver updates or releases.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c6d574ed-e8b9-4ae6-9e31-5594ffba7a01","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:05.384Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9676d3fd-70a3-4bbd-a473-e6bf629c32a1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
